f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307812 1113 #uuid "4f9a4e20-7e8e-527a-be2f-a1754c63ae1e" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307812 1145 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307814 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307815 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307816 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307817 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307818 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1041 "selection" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307818 13194139534755 true] [17592186307813 1110 #uuid "5f9c42fe-7ed4-530a-b364-e83a670e6de4" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307814 1034 "{e}^{2}" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534755 true] [17592186307814 1111 #uuid "f45327ce-b54d-586f-b76a-f2776bcc3c06" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307815 1034 "1" 13194139534755 true] [17592186307815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534755 true] [17592186307815 1111 #uuid "8174112b-33f7-56f9-b489-a475ee2f5365" 13194139534755 true